# Presentation and a later Three.js viewport

`web/src/ui/map/` and `web/src/ui/battle/` are the v1 picture. A Three.js viewport can replace them after the demo. The campaign already plays `GameSession`. Before starting that port, ask: should the viewport use React Three Fiber (R3F)? The HUD is already React; prefer R3F's declarative JSX over a large imperative vanilla Three.js file unless they choose vanilla. Do not put combat odds, legal moves, supply, or ownership rules in `MapView.tsx`, `mapRenderer.ts`, or battle canvas code. Derived layout (stack seats, counts, battle marks) comes from pure helpers over a snapshot.

Keep camera math (`viewMath.ts`, LOD) and spatial hit tests (which region or stack) free of `CanvasRenderingContext2D`, `drawImage`, `Path2D`, and `ctx.filter`, so a later camera matrix and raycast can reuse them.

HUD (turn bar, purchase, inspector, menus, war room) stays React. Do not couple it to pan, zoom, pointer pixels, or animation frames. Store those in `useRef`. Notify React only on discrete changes: selection, hover *label*, phase, modal open or close. Do not put pan/zoom deltas or 60 fps ticks in `useState`.

Faction colours are hex/RGB data, not runtime CSS on DOM nodes. Player-facing copy still says IO, never IPC.

Inkscape land masters and sprite plates are the source pictures. Do not bake DOM or SVG node assumptions into unit or faction tables.
